# ai.sh

A tiny AI assistant for your terminal, written in plain POSIX `sh`.
Ask a question or describe a task; it answers or suggests a shell command and runs it **only after you approve**.

- Works with **Anthropic**, **OpenAI** and **Ollama** (local, no API key)
- One-shot mode and an interactive **chat/agent mode** that can run several commands in a row
- Saved conversations (or `--temp-session` to save nothing)
- No dependencies besides `curl` and `jq`

```console
$ ai.sh "list files larger than 100MB"
$ ai.sh --chat
$ ai.sh -y "show disk usage per directory"      # auto-approve commands
```

## Install

Pick **one** way.

### Any Linux / macOS (bash, zsh, fish, dash, ...)

```sh
curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/snapsettle/ai.sh/main/install.sh | sh
```

No `curl`? Use `wget`:

```sh
wget -qO- https://raw.githubusercontent.com/snapsettle/ai.sh/main/install.sh | sh
```

This puts `ai.sh` (and a short alias `ai`) in `~/.local/bin` and checks the download against a checksum.
If `~/.local/bin` is not in your `PATH`, the installer prints the exact line to add for your shell.

You also need `jq` (`sudo apt install jq`, `brew install jq`, `sudo pacman -S jq`, ...).
Windows: use WSL.

Options (put them before `sh`):

| What | How |
| --- | --- |
| Install a specific version | `curl -fsSL .../install.sh \| AI_SH_VERSION=2026.10.25 sh` |
| Install somewhere else | `curl -fsSL .../install.sh \| AI_SH_INSTALL_DIR=/usr/local/bin sh` |
| Uninstall | `curl -fsSL .../install.sh \| sh -s -- --uninstall` |

### NixOS / Nix

Nix users never run `--update`. Nix replaces the whole package when you rebuild.

**With flakes (recommended)**: in your `flake.nix`:

```nix
{
  inputs = {
    nixpkgs.url = "github:NixOS/nixpkgs/nixos-unstable";
    ai-sh = {
      url = "github:snapsettle/ai.sh";
      inputs.nixpkgs.follows = "nixpkgs";
    };
  };

  outputs = { nixpkgs, ai-sh, ... }: {
    nixosConfigurations.YOUR-HOSTNAME = nixpkgs.lib.nixosSystem {
      modules = [
        ./configuration.nix
        { nixpkgs.overlays = [ ai-sh.overlays.default ]; }
      ];
    };
  };
}
```

then in `configuration.nix`:

```nix
{ pkgs, ... }: {
  environment.systemPackages = [ pkgs.ai-sh ];
}
```

Rebuild with `sudo nixos-rebuild switch --flake .` and the commands `ai.sh` and `ai` are available.
Home Manager works the same way: `home.packages = [ pkgs.ai-sh ];`.

**Without flakes**: in `configuration.nix`:

```nix
{ pkgs, ... }:
let
  src = builtins.fetchTarball "https://github.com/snapsettle/ai.sh/archive/refs/heads/main.tar.gz";
  ai-sh = pkgs.callPackage "${src}/package.nix" { inherit src; };
in
{
  environment.systemPackages = [ ai-sh ];
}
```

**Just try it** (nothing installed): `nix run github:snapsettle/ai.sh -- "your request"`
**Install for your user**: `nix profile install github:snapsettle/ai.sh`

## Update

| How you installed | How to update |
| --- | --- |
| One-liner / manual | `ai.sh --update` |
| Nix flake | `nix flake update ai-sh` then `sudo nixos-rebuild switch --flake .` |
| Nix without flakes | `sudo nixos-rebuild switch` (Nix re-downloads about once an hour) |

`ai.sh --update` downloads the newest release, verifies its checksum and syntax, then replaces itself.
At most once a day ai.sh quietly checks for a newer version and tells you if there is one.
Turn that off with `export SH_AI_NO_UPDATE_CHECK=1`.
Running `ai.sh --update` on a Nix install does nothing except explain the Nix way.

## First run

Run `ai.sh` once in a terminal. A short setup asks which provider(s) you use and for an API key
(or reads `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` / `OPENAI_API_KEY` from your environment). Change it later with `ai.sh --reconfig`.

## Usage

```text
ai.sh [options] "request"          One-shot mode
ai.sh --chat [options] ["message"] Conversation mode
```

| Option | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| `-y`, `--yolo` | Flip auto-approval of commands (off by default). Deleting chats always asks. |
| `--chat`, `--convo` | Interactive conversation mode |
| `--temp-session` | Chat without saving anything to disk |
| `--update` | Update to the newest release (not for Nix) |
| `--reconfig` | Change settings |
| `--reset`, `--reset-config`, `--reset-convos` | Delete everything / settings / saved chats |
| `--version`, `-h`, `--help` | Version / help |

Inside a chat: `/exit` leaves (chat is kept), `/delete` leaves and deletes it, `/help` lists commands.

Environment variables: `SH_AI_PROVIDER`, `SH_AI_MODEL`, `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`, `OPENAI_API_KEY`,
`OLLAMA_HOST`, `SH_AI_DIR`, `SH_AI_NO_UPDATE_CHECK`, `NO_COLOR`.

Settings and chats live in `~/.cache/ai.sh` (or `$SH_AI_DIR`). The config file is readable only by you.

### Safety

- Commands are shown and **need your approval** unless you use `-y`.
- Even with `-y`, anything that does not look like a real command, and every chat deletion, still asks first.
- You are running commands an AI wrote. Read them, especially with `-y`.

## Versions

Versions are dates: `2026.10.25`, or `2026.10.25.1` for a second release on the same day.
Every release is on the [Releases page](https://github.com/snapsettle/ai.sh/releases).
`ai.sh --version` shows what you have. A copy run straight from a git checkout shows `dev`.

## For maintainers: how releases work (nothing to do by hand)

1. Edit `ai.sh` and push to `main`.
2. GitHub Actions (`.github/workflows/release.yml`) checks the script, picks today's date as the version,
   writes it into `ai.sh`, commits it back, tags `vYYYY.MM.DD`, and publishes a Release.
3. One-liner users get it via `ai.sh --update`. Nix users get it when they update their input / rebuild.

One-time GitHub setup: **Settings → Actions → General → Workflow permissions → Read and write permissions**.
After each release the bot adds one commit, so run `git pull` before your next push.
Never edit the `VERSION=` line yourself.